const faq = [
  { q: 'Where does the Brown-necked Raven live?', a: 'The Brown-necked Raven (Corvus ruficollis) occupies arid and semi-arid zones across a vast area from the Cape Verde Islands and North Africa east through the Middle East and Arabian Peninsula to Pakistan, India (Sind and Kutch), and Central Asia (Afghanistan, Iran, and neighbouring regions). It is essentially the raven of the desert and steppe zone of the Old World, occupying the habitats too arid for the Common Raven.' },
  { q: 'Does the Brown-necked Raven overlap with the Common Raven?', a: 'The two species meet at the margins of their ranges. In North Africa, Common Ravens occur in the Atlas Mountains while Brown-necked Ravens occupy the adjacent desert lowlands. In the Canary Islands, both occur on Lanzarote and Fuerteventura — one of the few places where the two species co-exist. In this overlap zone, careful attention to size, bill, and head shape distinguishes them. The Common Raven is larger and heavier-billed; the Brown-necked Raven is slimmer.' },
  { q: 'Is the Brown-necked Raven found in Israel?', a: 'Yes. The Brown-necked Raven is resident in the arid Negev Desert and Arava Valley of Israel — one of the most accessible locations for Western observers to see the species. It coexists here with the Fan-tailed Raven, which also occurs in the Negev and Judean Desert. Both species can be seen at Mitzpe Ramon and around the cliff faces of the Negev highlands.' },
];
